chapter BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK:
    narrator "Bridgitte is outside her house, checking the edge on a skinning knife."
    narrator "She glances up as you approach."

    bridgitte "Back again?"
    bridgitte "If it's training, say so. If it's questions, ask."

    choice:
        "Ask about adventurer ranks." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_RANKS
        "Ask about her life as an adventurer." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_ADVENTURER_LIFE
        "Ask why she left adventuring behind." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_LEFT_ADVENTURING
        "Ask what her job is in the village." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_VILLAGE_JOB
        "Ask about the village." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_VILLAGE
        "Leave." -> BRIDGITTE_LORE_END


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_RANKS:
    player "Can you explain adventurer ranks?"

    bridgitte "You start at G."
    bridgitte "Then F, E, D, C, B, A, and S."
    bridgitte "Most people who call themselves adventurers never climb past E or D."
    bridgitte "C-rank is where people start treating you like a professional instead of a hopeful fool with boots."
    bridgitte "B-rank means you've survived enough serious work that the guild trusts you with ugly problems."
    bridgitte "A-rank means kingdoms notice when you cross a border."
    bridgitte "S-rank means everyone notices."

    player "Are there ranks above S?"

    bridgitte "Technically."
    bridgitte "Double-S exists."
    bridgitte "Very, very rare. The kind of rare people argue about in taverns because most have never seen one."
    bridgitte "Triple-S is different."
    bridgitte "That's less an adventurer rank and more a warning label."

    player "For monsters?"

    bridgitte "Mostly."
    bridgitte "Monsters use the same scale, more or less."
    bridgitte "A G-rank monster should be manageable for a trained G-rank adventurer."
    bridgitte "Should be."
    bridgitte "Ranks weigh strength, danger to civilians, magical power, habits, terrain, all of it."
    bridgitte "Numbers help. They don't replace judgment."

    player "So even a low-rank monster can still be dangerous."

    bridgitte "A kitchen knife can kill someone."
    bridgitte "A ditch can kill someone."
    bridgitte "Rank tells you what a thing is expected to be."
    bridgitte "It doesn't promise you get to be stupid around it."

    bridgitte "Remember that when you see the coyote."
    bridgitte "Low rank doesn't mean harmless."
    bridgitte "It means you're allowed to learn before the world gets crueler."

    goto BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_ADVENTURER_LIFE:
    player "What was your life like as an adventurer?"

    bridgitte "Loud."
    bridgitte "Cold."
    bridgitte "Hungry more often than the songs admit."

    player "That sounds miserable."

    bridgitte "It was wonderful."
    bridgitte "Sometimes."

    narrator "For a moment, Bridgitte's guarded expression softens."

    bridgitte "I was part of a party called Bringadine."
    bridgitte "Good people. Better fighters."
    bridgitte "We took work across half the roads between here and places you probably haven't heard of yet."

    player "What happened to them?"

    bridgitte "Nothing bad, last I heard."
    bridgitte "They didn't disband."
    bridgitte "I left."

    player "And they kept going without you?"

    bridgitte "They did."
    bridgitte "Last word I had, they were in the Omega Empire."
    bridgitte "Promoted to A-rank, too."

    player "Were you proud of them?"

    bridgitte "Of course I was."
    bridgitte "You don't stop caring about people just because you're no longer walking beside them."

    player "What did you do in the party?"

    bridgitte "I was the sub-tank."

    player "What does that mean?"

    bridgitte "A headache to explain before you even know how not to trip over your own guard."
    bridgitte "For now, understand this."
    bridgitte "Some people stand in front."
    bridgitte "Some people strike from behind."
    bridgitte "Some people watch the places where the formation is about to break."
    bridgitte "That was me."

    player "Do you miss it?"

    bridgitte "Some days."
    bridgitte "Some days I miss the road."
    bridgitte "Some days I remember exactly why a roof, a warm bed, and villagers who bring too many turnips can feel like a blessing."

    goto BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_LEFT_ADVENTURING:
    player "Why did you leave adventuring?"

    narrator "Bridgitte's hands still."

    bridgitte "Reasons."

    player "You don't want to talk about it."

    bridgitte "No."

    player "Did something happen?"

    bridgitte "Something always happens."
    bridgitte "People don't leave a life like that because one morning the porridge tastes wrong."

    player "Were you hurt?"

    bridgitte "Not in a way you'll see."

    narrator "She resumes working the blade across the whetstone."

    bridgitte "Listen."
    bridgitte "There are questions a person can answer."
    bridgitte "There are questions a person can survive answering."
    bridgitte "And there are questions that turn the past into a room with no door."

    player "I'm sorry. I didn't mean to push."

    bridgitte "You pushed a little."
    bridgitte "But not cruelly."
    bridgitte "That's why I'm not throwing this whetstone at you."

    player "Will you tell me someday?"

    bridgitte "Maybe."
    bridgitte "If someday becomes the sort of day where it matters."

    bridgitte "For now, take the answer I gave you."
    bridgitte "Reasons."
    bridgitte "And leave the rest where it is."

    goto BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_VILLAGE_JOB:
    player "What exactly do you do here?"

    bridgitte "I hunt."

    player "And handle monsters."

    bridgitte "Yes."

    player "And people come to you when something dangerous gets close."

    bridgitte "Also yes."

    player "That sounds like a job."

    bridgitte "It sounds like work."
    bridgitte "A job has a title, a wage, and someone important pretending she invented the need for it."

    player "Then what are you?"

    bridgitte "Useful."

    narrator "She says it plainly, without pride."

    bridgitte "This village doesn't have a quest board."
    bridgitte "No posted contracts. No guild clerk. No tidy slips of paper saying three coyotes are worth five copper and a bowl of stew."
    bridgitte "But the work still exists."
    bridgitte "Predators wander too close. Burrows spread. Monsters come down from the rough ground when weather, hunger, or whatever else drives them."

    player "And you handle all of it?"

    bridgitte "As much as I can."
    bridgitte "Most of it is small."
    bridgitte "G-rank and F-rank nuisances. E-rank if the day wants to be irritating."
    bridgitte "D-rank outbreaks happen, but not often."

    player "Can the villagers fight?"

    bridgitte "Some can swing a tool hard enough to make a wolf reconsider."
    bridgitte "That is not the same as fighting monsters."
    bridgitte "Farmers should be farming. Children should be growing. Old people should be complaining in peace."
    bridgitte "So yes."
    bridgitte "When teeth come near the fences, I handle it."

    player "And now I help."

    bridgitte "Now you help."
    bridgitte "Not because I want a servant."
    bridgitte "Because you were given kindness, and kindness should teach your hands to become useful."

    goto BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_VILLAGE:
    player "What's this village like?"

    bridgitte "Quiet."
    bridgitte "Harsh."
    bridgitte "Mostly honest."
    bridgitte "Not much to do unless you like mending, hauling, chopping, milking, smoking meat, counting hens, chasing hens, or apologizing to the woman whose garden your hens destroyed."

    player "You make it sound boring."

    bridgitte "It is boring."
    bridgitte "That's part of why I like it."

    player "Because adventuring was worse?"

    bridgitte "Because adventuring was louder."
    bridgitte "You learn to appreciate mornings where the worst thing waiting for you is a broken latch."
    bridgitte "No ambush."
    bridgitte "No blood trail."
    bridgitte "No guild letter sealed in black wax."
    bridgitte "Just a latch."

    player "But it's still dangerous here."

    bridgitte "Everywhere is dangerous."
    bridgitte "Cities hide knives behind smiles."
    bridgitte "Roads hide bandits behind trees."
    bridgitte "Villages hide danger behind routine."
    bridgitte "You get used to the same paths, the same fences, the same sounds at night."
    bridgitte "Then one sound changes, and if nobody notices, something gets through."

    player "Is that why the villagers love you?"

    bridgitte "They love me because I fix things and don't ask too many questions."
    bridgitte "And because I once punched a boar hard enough that the chief still tells the story wrong."

    player "Did you really punch a boar?"

    bridgitte "Yes."
    bridgitte "Hard enough to do what the chief claims? No."
    bridgitte "Let her have it. Chiefs need stories. It keeps people listening when there is real work to assign."

    narrator "She looks toward the village lanes, where smoke rises from low chimneys."

    bridgitte "It's not much."
    bridgitte "But it is alive."
    bridgitte "Places like this stay alive because people do small hard things every day."
    bridgitte "You'll learn that if you stay long enough."

    goto BRIDGITTE_REPEATABLE_TALK


chapter BRIDGITTE_LORE_END:
    player "That's all for now."

    bridgitte "Good."
    bridgitte "Too much talking dulls the edge."

    player "Of the dagger?"

    bridgitte "Of the person holding it."

    return
